-- EzTongues static data: the languages, their letter ciphers and lore phrases, and who knows what by default.
-- The word pairs are in Dictionary.lua (generated by tools/build_dictionary.py, which also reads this file).
--
-- FROZEN: every line ever sent must keep decoding. Never change a language's key, consonants, vowels, markers,
-- doubleVowel or doubleLast, and never remove or edit a phrase: add new phrases or languages only (and rerun the
-- tool, which checks that new phrase words aren't already taken). tests/smoke.lua pins sample encodings.
local _, ns = ...

ns.ERA = select(4, GetBuildInfo()) < 16000

-- The cipher maps English consonants, most common first, onto `consonants` letter by letter, and the vowels
-- (in this order) onto `vowels`: consonants stay consonants and vowels stay vowels, so ciphered words can be said.
ns.CONSONANTS = "tnshrdlcmwfgypbvkjxqz"
ns.VOWELS = "eaoiu"

-- One entry per language, in the order /tongue list shows them (and the tool pairs words: first come, first served).
--   key        saved in EzTonguesCharDB and typed after /tongue; also the Dictionary.lua key
--   id         Blizzard's Languages.db2 ID, whose LanguageWords the dictionary draws from
--   markers    a ciphered word (a name, a rare word) starts with one of these and an apostrophe: Ael'Bab. No
--              dictionary word starts that way, so the decoder knows which path to take. Letters only.
--   doubleVowel  ciphered words double their first vowel (Ael'Baab); the decoder drops it again
--   doubleLast   ciphered words ending in one of these letters double it (Dag'Grukk)
--   phrases    { English, native }: whole well-known lines from Warcraft Wiki, swapped whole (longest first)
--              when the English is typed all lower case, Sentence case or ALL CAPS. Natives use one leading capital.
